{
  "id": "navigation/references/13_override_calls",
  "topic": "navigation",
  "subtopic": "references",
  "title": "Calls through overridden methods",
  "summary": "Find references on an override includes the calls made through the methods it overrides, which may dispatch to it",
  "prose": "`shape.area()` on a `Shape&` may run `Circle::area`, so it is among the\nreferences of the override. A call through `Circle` never runs\n`Shape::area`, so it is not among the references of the method overridden;\nnor is `Shape::area()` in the override's own body, which names the method\noverridden, or `circle.Shape::scale(factor)` in a template, which names one\nof the overloads overridden before any instantiation picks it.",
  "status": "supported",
  "issues": [
    "clangd#1558"
  ],
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "main.cpp",
      "text": "struct Shape {\n    virtual double area() const {\n        return 0.0;\n    }\n\n    virtual double scale(int factor) const {\n        return factor;\n    }\n\n    virtual double scale(double factor) const {\n        return factor;\n    }\n};\n\nstruct Circle : Shape {\n    double area() const override {\n        return Shape::area() + 3.0;\n    }\n\n    double scale(int factor) const override {\n        return factor * 2.0;\n    }\n};\n\ndouble through_base(const Shape& shape) {\n    return shape.area() + shape.scale(2);\n}\n\ndouble through_circle(const Circle& circle) {\n    return circle.area();\n}\n\ntemplate <typename T>\ndouble base_scale(const Circle& circle, T factor) {\n    return circle.Shape::scale(factor);\n}\n",
